On Tue, 21 Dec 2004 csharpmajor@gmail.com wrote:

> I don't understand these instructions.  People seem to be telling me
> that once I unzip the gz with the source in it I will get a folder
> containing what I want.  I have downloaded the source from 3 different
> places.  None of them contain a folder of any description, just the
> nethack-343-src file.  Can someone just email me the rest of the stuff
> that was *supposed* to be in there?  Or something?

If it is a .tar.gz file, this means that the contents are first subjected
to tar and then to gz. After decompressing with gzip, you have to untar
the resulting file. The command to do this is
	tar -xf (filename)

> There is this other thing that's bugging me.  There is no executable in
> my top folder for WinGW, just a bunch of folders.  Have I done
> something wrong?

The executables are probably in subfolders called bin or usr\bin.

> Do I HAVE to use gzip and tar?  Can't I just use
> winrar?  Oh, and what does -xvf do?

If winrar can work with .tar.gz files, yes, you can use it.

> And to top it all off, Windows XP seems to have been designed to annoy
> its users.  I can't find ANYTHING! *wail*

If you want to look for a specific file in some subtree of a directory,
use
	dir filename /s
For example,
	dir nhsetup.bat /s
or
	dir *setup*.bat /s

